    {
      "id": "event-handler-behavior-instructions",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Handler Contract Sketch",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": 1580
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 300,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        },
        {
          "id": "guided-by",
          "key": "guided-by",
          "label": "guided by",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180,
          "role": "relationship",
          "allowedEdgeTypes": [
            "reference"
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "relationship": true,
            "role": "relationship"
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "event-handler-behavior"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Event handler contract\n\nAn Event Handler is the binding in **when this happens, do that**.\n\n## Event\n\n- Exactly one source node and one event key, for example a Property's `property.changed`.\n- An enabled switch. A disabled handler remains visible and says so.\n- Every matching occurrence invokes the Method.\n- Several Events that need the same reaction use several Event Handlers that reference the same Method.\n\n## Method\n\n- Exactly one referenced Method.\n- The Method receives the complete occurrence as documented input: payload, source, event key, occurrence and cause identifiers, and relevant delivery context.\n- The Method owns decisions, executable logic, mutation authority, and declared effects.\n- The handler may display a derived, read-only summary of the Method effects.\n\n## Runtime guarantees\n\nTwilite owns deterministic delivery order, loop protection, time limits, failure isolation, and bridge authorization. These are delivery-system guarantees rather than fields on every handler.\n\n## Diagnostics\n\nThe handler may show live last-run outcome, time, duration, and error. This is runtime state by default. A graph that needs durable history must explicitly log it.\n\n## Test it\n\nAuthors should be able to invoke the Event Handler with a sample occurrence in dry-run mode and review the Method's proposed effects.\n\nThe durable authored contract is one Event, one Method, and an enabled state."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        }
      },
      "handles": [
